The Nautilus DevOps team aims to containerize various applications following a recent meeting with the application development team. 
They intend to conduct testing with the following steps:

Install docker-ce and docker compose packages on App Server 1.

Initiate the docker service.

SOLUTION
 Prerequisites: Verify Your OS
The installation method depends on your server's operating system. The search results include instructions for both RHEL/CentOS and Ubuntu/Debian.

You can check your OS with:

bash
cat /etc/os-release
🛠️ Installation Steps
Option A: For RHEL / CentOS / Rocky / Alma Linux
If your App Server 1 runs a Red Hat-based distribution, follow these steps:

Install the repository management tool:

bash
sudo dnf -y install dnf-plugins-core
Add the official Docker repository:

bash
sudo dnf config-manager --add-repo https://download.docker.com/linux/rhel/docker-ce.repo
(Note: You can also use the CentOS repo URL, which often works for compatible distributions: https://download.docker.com/linux/centos/docker-ce.repo) 

Install Docker Engine and the Compose plugin:

bash
sudo dnf install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin
This command installs the Docker daemon, CLI, containerd runtime, and the Compose plugin (which provides the docker compose command) .

Option B: For Ubuntu / Debian
If your server runs Ubuntu or Debian:

Install prerequisites:

bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install ca-certificates curl
Add Docker's official GPG key and repository:

bash
sudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings
sudo curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc
sudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc

echo \
  "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu \
  $(. /etc/os-release && echo "$VERSION_CODENAME") stable" | \
  sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null
Install Docker Engine and the Compose plugin:

bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin
```[citation:3][citation:15]
▶️ Start the Docker Service
After the packages are installed, start the Docker service and enable it to launch at boot:

bash
# Start Docker and enable it on boot
sudo systemctl enable --now docker
If you prefer to start it separately:

bash
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker

✅ Verification
Confirm that Docker is installed and running correctly:

Check the service status:

bash
sudo systemctl status docker
Run the hello-world container:

bash
sudo docker run hello-world
Verify the Docker Compose plugin:

bash
docker compose version
```[citation:3][citation:8][citation:18]  
